\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 4.3 Pathway-Level Convergence Across the Combination\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Reading Table 2 by column rather than by row makes the multi-target rationale for this combination explicit: TNF/IL-6/NF-kB-linked inflammatory signalling and Nrf2-dependent antioxidant response are each independently reported for all five compounds at 'reported' strength or above, while AKT1/PI3K signalling and AMPK-centred metabolic signalling are each independently reported, at 'reported' strength or above, for at least three of the five. This pattern of independently documented convergence across compound-specific literatures - quercetin and mangiferin contributing the strongest documented engagement with growth-factor/survival signalling, anacardic acid contributing a structurally distinctive enzyme-inhibitory mechanism, and the bamboo-derived orientin and vitexin contributing consistently to the oxidative-stress and inflammatory axes - is the central, literature-grounded hypothesis this article puts forward; it is offered as a synthesis of what is already documented, not as a network pharmacology result in its own right.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 4.4 What Independent Network Construction and Docking Would Still Need to Confirm\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 The pattern in Section 4.3 is a hypothesis precisely because it has not been confirmed by a network built from this specific five-compound set, nor by docking any of the five compounds against any of the five targets/pathways discussed. A genuine STRING/Cytoscape network (Section 3.4.2-3.4.3) could surface additional or different hub targets not anticipated by this compound-by-compound literature synthesis; a genuine enrichment analysis (Section 3.4.4) could show that some of the documented associations summarised in Table 2, while individually real, do not in fact dominate the network topology once all predicted targets are considered together; and genuine docking (Section 3.4.5) could show that any one compound's predicted binding to a given target, however well documented qualitatively, is in fact weak relative to the others once an actual binding-affinity estimate is computed. None of these possibilities can be ruled out by literature synthesis alone.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 4.5 What This Synthesis Would Mean, If Independently Confirmed\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 If the pattern in Section 4.3 were independently confirmed by the computational framework in Section 3.4, it would support a multi-target profile in which quercetin and mangiferin contribute the strongest engagement with growth-factor and survival signalling (AKT1) and inflammatory hub nodes (TNF/IL-6/RELA), anacardic acid contributes complementary anti-inflammatory engagement consistent with its documented enzyme-inhibitory activity, and the bamboo-derived C-glycosylflavones orientin and vitexin contribute relatively more toward the oxidative-stress and energy-sensing axis (NFE2L2/PRKAA1) - a division of labour across the three botanical sources that would echo, at the level of network pharmacology, the same complementary-mechanism logic this journal has previously discussed for a different bamboo-leaf combination (Korie, 2026). Whether this documented-literature pattern holds once a network is actually constructed and docking is actually run is precisely the question that executing the framework in Section 3.4 - and not this preliminary synthesis - would answer.\par
\pard\qj\b\fs28\sb240\sa120 5. Discussion\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 5.1 Why Publish a Preliminary Synthesis Before New Computation\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Network pharmacology and molecular docking studies have become common enough in the natural-products literature that the methodological template itself - which databases to query, in what order, with which thresholds - is now reasonably well established, even though individual papers rarely spell every step out in enough detail for independent replication, and fewer still make explicit how much of their eventual hub list could already have been anticipated from the separate literatures on each compound. Publishing a careful secondary synthesis first, decoupled from any newly computed result, serves a narrower but genuine purpose: it gives other researchers, particularly those working with limited computational resources or just beginning to apply these tools to African ethnobotanical material, a citable, literature-grounded starting hypothesis and a complete, named, reproducible protocol (Section 3.4) for testing it, rather than asking them to start from nothing. This is the same logic that justified publishing a literature-synthesis-only paper elsewhere in this journal (Korie, 2026): doing the preparatory, hypothesis-shaping work openly and honestly, clearly labelled as preparatory, before the resource-intensive primary computational work begins.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 5.2 Keeping Secondary Synthesis Distinct from Primary Computation\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Network pharmacology and docking studies carry a specific, well-documented risk: because the computational pipeline produces numbers - node counts, centrality scores, binding energies - that look identical in format whether or not the underlying analysis was actually run with care, it is comparatively easy for a literature synthesis like this one to drift into presenting its qualitative, secondary-source-derived claims as if they carried the same evidentiary weight as a freshly computed result. This article was deliberately structured to avoid that drift: Table 2 uses only qualitative documentation-strength labels (well documented, reported, limited evidence) rather than any numerical centrality score, p-value, or binding energy, precisely because no computation that would produce such a number was performed for this compound set; and the methodological notice in the Abstract and at the start of the document repeat the same distinction in case a reader arrives at Section 4 without having read the earlier sections. Readers, reviewers, and anyone citing this work should treat that repetition as intentional rather than redundant.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 5.3 Limitations\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 This article's limitations follow directly from its method: it is a synthesis of secondary literature, not a primary computational or experimental study, and its conclusions are only as strong as the literature search and synthesis judgement behind them. The literature search underlying Section 4 was conducted by a single author rather than through a systematic, pre-registered review protocol with independent dual screening, so omissions are possible; a compound-target association rated 'limited evidence' in Table 2 may simply reflect a gap in the sources consulted rather than a genuine absence of documented activity. More fundamentally, no degree of literature convergence, however consistent, is a substitute for an actual target-prediction query, network construction, enrichment analysis, or docking run against this specific five-compound set - a genuine computational run, specified in Section 3.4, could surface a substantially different hub list or show that a qualitatively well-documented association does not in fact dominate the network topology once all predicted targets are considered together.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 5.4 Staged Roadmap From Secondary Synthesis to Validated Findings\b0\i0\par
