\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 5.5 Relevance to Nigerian and African Computational Pharmacology Capacity\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Every tool named in Section 3.4 - PubChem, ChEMBL, SwissTargetPrediction, STRING, Cytoscape, the relevant R or Python enrichment packages, AutoDock Vina, and the Protein Data Bank - is free to access and runs on modest computing hardware, which makes this kind of framework genuinely usable by researchers and graduate students working in Nigerian and other African institutions without specialist wet-laboratory funding or high-performance computing infrastructure. Mangifera indica, Anacardium occidentale, and Bambusa species are all common, inexpensive, and widely cultivated across Nigeria, which lowers the practical barrier to eventually pairing any computational findings with locally sourced extracts for the in vitro and in vivo confirmation work described in Table 3. Making the methodology explicit and freely reusable, and grounding the starting hypothesis in real secondary literature rather than treating it as a black box behind a results table, is itself a small contribution toward building that capacity.\par
\pard\qj\b\fs28\sb240\sa120 6. Conclusion\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa160 This article undertakes a preliminary investigation of multi-target synergy between Anacardiaceae leaf compounds (mangiferin and quercetin from Mangifera indica; anacardic acid from Anacardium occidentale) and Poaceae leaf compounds (orientin and vitexin from Bambusa spp.), built on an extensive synthesis of secondary, previously published literature documenting each compound's individual molecular targets and pathway associations, and specifies a complete computational framework for converting that synthesis into a primary result. It deliberately stops short of claiming any newly computed finding: no target-prediction query, network construction, enrichment analysis, or docking run was independently performed for this specific five-compound set, and every claim in Section 4 is traced to its basis in existing secondary literature for exactly that reason.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 What this article offers instead is a citable, literature-grounded preliminary synthesis - showing that TNF/IL-6/NF-kB-linked inflammatory signalling and Nrf2-dependent antioxidant response are independently documented for all five compounds, and that AKT1/PI3K and AMPK-centred signalling are independently documented for at least three of the five - together with a named, step-by-step protocol (Section 3.4) for testing that hypothesis computationally, and a staged roadmap (Table 3) showing exactly what primary computational and laboratory work remains before any claim about this combination's actual multi-target mechanism could be responsibly made. Whether mangiferin, quercetin, anacardic acid, orientin, and vitexin genuinely converge on these targets within a single constructed network, and whether that convergence holds up under docking, is an open, answerable, and inexpensive question for the next researcher - in Nigeria or elsewhere - willing to run the framework in Section 3.4 for real.\par
